              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒮
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            such that
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The relation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo &lt;
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lt
                belongs to
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝒮
                          %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}_2
            %li
              %p
                The elements of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒮
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}_1
                are precisely finite unions of points and intervals in
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
          %p
            Here an interval can mean a set of the form
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi I
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ,
                        %mi b
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi R
                    %mo :
                    %mi a
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi x
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I_{a, b} = \{x \in R: a \lt x \lt b\}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi I
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mi a
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi R
                    %mo :
                    %mi x
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I_{\downarrow a} = \{x \in R: x \lt a\}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi I
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                        %mi a
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi R
                    %mo :
                    %mi a
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I_{\uparrow a} = \{x \in R: a \lt x\}
          %h2#commentary Commentary
          %p
            A structure on a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            can be thought of as the collection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒮
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                    %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒮
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S} = \bigcup_n \mathcal{S}_n
            of sets that are definable with respect to a one-sorted first-order language
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            with a given interpretation in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒮
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}_n
            is the collection of subsets of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi R
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R^n
            which are defined by
            = succeed "-ary" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            contains a relation symbol
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo &lt;
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lt
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo &lt;
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lt
            is interpreted in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            as a dense linear order without endpoints.
          %p
            The o-minimality condition places a sharp restriction on which subsets of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            can be defined in the language. Essentially, it means that the only definable subsets of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            are those which are definable in terms of constants and the predicates
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo &lt;
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lt
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo =
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} =
          %p
            The archetypal example of an o-minimal structure is that of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semi-algebraic+set"} semi-algebraic set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            (which form a structure due to the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semialgebraic+set"} Tarski-Seidenberg theorem
          %p
            Remarkably, quite a lot can be said about the structure of definable sets in an o-minimal structure over
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tame+topology"} tame topology
            %em topologie modérée
            ).
          %h2#ominimal_theories O-minimal theories
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory"} theory
            is o-minimal if every model
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            is an o-minimal structure.
          %h2#relation_to_number_theory_and_arithmetic_geometry Relation to number theory and arithmetic geometry
          %p
            The theory of o-minimal structures has applications to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/number+theory"} number theory
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arithmetic+geometry"} arithmetic geometry
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Tsimerman19"} Tsimerman19
          %h2#links Links
          %ul
            %li
              wikipedia
              %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/O-minimal_theory"} o-minimal theory
          %h2#references References
          %p General:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Michel Coste,
                %em An introduction to O-minimal geometry
                , Lecture notes Pisa 1999. (
                %a{:href => "http://perso.univ-rennes1.fr/michel.coste/polyens/OMIN.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lou+van+den+Dries"}> Lou van den Dries
                ,
                %em Exponential rings, exponential polynomials and exponential functions
                , Pacific Journal of Mathematics
                %strong 113
                no.1 (1984) pp.51–66. (
                %a{:href => "http://projecteuclid.org/download/pdf_1/euclid.pjm/1102709376"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                Lou van den Dries,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Tame topology and O-minimal structures
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexandre+Grothendieck"}> Alexandre Grothendieck
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Esquisse d’un Programme
            %li
              %p
                Alessandro Berarducci,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Definable groups in o-minimal structures
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.dm.unipi.it/~dinasso/marian2004/berarducci.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Cohomology of groups in o-minimal structures: acyclicity of the infinitesimal subgroup
                = succeed ":3" do
                  %strong 74
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=2548466"} MR2548466
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://projecteuclid.org/euclid.jsl/1245158089"} euclid
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.2178/jsl/1245158089"} doi
            %li
              %p
                Alessandro Berarducci,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em O-minimal spectra, infinitesimal subgroups and cohomology
                %strong 72
                (2007), no. 4, pp. 1177–1193,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=2371198"} MR2371198
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://projecteuclid.org/euclid.jsl/1203350779"} euclid
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.2178/jsl/1203350779"} doi
            %li
              %p
                M. Edmundo, G. O. Jones, N. J. Peatfield,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Sheaf cohomology in o-minimal structures
                %strong 6
                (2006), no. 2, pp. 163–179,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=2317425"} MR2317425
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1142/S0219061306000566"} doi
            %li
              %p
                Mario J. Edmundo, Luca Prelli,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Invariance of o-minimal cohomology with definably compact supports
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1205.6124"} arxiv/1205.6124
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.5802/cml.17"} doi
            %li
              %p
                M. J. Edmundo, N. J. Peatfield,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em O-minimal Čech cohomology
                %a{:href => "https://repositorioaberto.uab.pt/bitstream/10400.2/2839/1/ConstCech.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                Ricardo Bianconi, Rodrigo Figueiredo,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em O-minimal de Rham cohomology
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.05485"} arxiv/1904.05485
            %li
              %p
                Olivier Le Gal, Jean-Philippe Rolin,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    An o-minimal structure which does not admit
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi C
                            %mn ∞
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{\infty }
                    cellular decomposition
                = succeed ":2" do
                  %strong 59
                %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=2521427"} MR2521427
                %a{:href => "http://www.zentralblatt-math.org/zmath/en/search/?q=an:1193.03065"} Zbl 1193.03065
                %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item?id=AIF_2009__59_2_543_0"} numdam
            %li
              %p
                Thomas Scanlon,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Algebraic differential equations from covering maps
                %strong 330
                (2018) 1071-1100
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.aim.2018.03.008"} doi
            %li
              %p
                Benjamin Bakker, Yohan Brunebarbe, Jacob Tsimerman,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em o-minimal GAGA and a conjecture of Griffiths
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.12230"} arxiv/1811.12230
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reid+Barton"}> Reid Barton
                , Johan Commelin,
                %em Model categories for o-minimal geometry
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.11952"}> arXiv:2108.11952
                )
            %li#Tsimerman19
              %p
                Jacob Tsimerman,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Functional transcendence results and arithmetic applications
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1142/9789813272880_0062"} doi
                %a{:href => "https://eta.impa.br/dl/091.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            In Physics, the concept of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tame+topology"} tame topology
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/o-minimal+structures"} o-minimal structures
            appears in a proposal for a finiteness condition in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QFT"} QFT
            in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Douglas"}> Michael Douglas
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thomas+Grimm"} Thomas Grimm
                %em The Tameness of Quantum Field Theory, Part I–Amplitudes.
                (2022)(
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.10057"}> arXiv:2210.10057
                ).
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Douglas"}> Michael Douglas
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thomas+Grimm"} Thomas Grimm
                %em The Tameness of Quantum Field Theory, Part II–Structures and CFTs
                (2023) (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.04275"}> arXiv:2302.04275
                ).
